      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it from scientific, environmental, and biological perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Composition\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water is polar (having a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side). This allows it to dissolve more substances than any other liquid, which is why it is vital for chemical reactions in nature and industry.\n\n### 2. Physical States of Matter\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n* **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, which fills the oceans, rivers, and rain.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Uniquely, solid water (ice) is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F), existing invisibly in the air as humidity or visibly as clouds and steam.\n\n### 3. Importance to Life\nWater is the foundation of all known life. \n* **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. Cells require water to function, transport nutrients, flush out waste, and regulate temperature.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water habitats (oceans, rivers, lakes) are home to the majority of Earth's plant and animal species.\n\n### 4. Earth’s Global Presence\n* **The \"Blue Planet\":** Water covers approximately **71% of the Earth’s surface**, mostly contained within the five great oceans.\n* **Scarcity:** Despite how common it is, only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater** (non-salty), and most of that is locked away in glaciers,"
